aving they want  a job that's not  so stressful when it comes to ppe like  gloves  lexington regional only has about a two  week supply  during the spring surge marsh used a  lifeline  getting shipments from the non-profit  angel flight central  which serves rural hospitals in 10  states and it was a lifesaver  literally a lifesaver and i think that  we'll probably have to reach out to them  again here soon  these are masks these are n95s these are  n95s  in new york supplies come in here about  30 deliveries a day actually come in  here from all over the globe  north wells 85 thousand square foot  warehouse  is overflowing with ppe  northwell didn't need to rely on the  federal government's dwindling stockpile  it uses an automated system to respond  to each hospital's needs  this must have been massively reassuring  to your staff  we were able to tell them we have enough  supplies we can assure you that  that was enormous when you talk to some  of the staff  are any of them telling you i can't do  this again  i have not held much of that at all i  haven't had anybody come to me or talk  to me  and say i wouldn't do this again they  recognized  that they saved so many lives  we all go into healthcare primarily  because we want to make a difference and  so  it is an honor and an obligation and  you know a duty and a privilege to be  able to be in this building  northwell health where i am on staff  estimates it has the resources to ride  out a year of projected lost revenue  the reality is states and hospitals are  engaging in bidding wars against each  other for ppe  and for medical workers smaller  hospitals like lexington regional cannot  compete and often lose out  the hospital tells us it only has 12  days of  cash left on hand wow so tara with all  these cutbacks and with the staff  shortages and losses what are doctors  and nurses  doing to keep themselves in this  all-important fight  well tony both ceos really stress that  staff is their most valuable resource  and asset and there are things that  leadership can do in terms of being  there on the front lines walking the  halls  talking to the staff having open lines  of communication and then little things  like lexington regional ceo told us  they brought in tacos one saturday night  to help feed the staff  north wall which obviously has bigger  more resources was able to provide  things like daycare  hotel rooms for staff workers so they  didn't have to go home and expose their  family  counseling services but both of the ceos  said that really what helps motivate the  staff are the victories are the  survivors uh you know at my hospital  lennox hill which is affiliated with  part of northwell  they would play the song here comes the  sun every time a patient  was taken off a ventilator throughout  the loudspeakers in the hospital every  time a patient was discharged  and hearing that song really helps  motivate the staff  and the patients that is a beautiful  anecdote there i love that thank you  very much doctor tara noula we  appreciate it
 

